I've managed -4 naked a couple of times. You are
ignoring how incredibly much cheaper it is to buy
protection at XL:1. Since it isn't terribly
infrequent for my PCs to hit MinesTown, clean out
the shops, and end up with 9000 to 12000 zorkmids
(the average take from six shops, four in Mines, two
in Dlvl:1 to Dlvl:4), there's _lots_ of money to
burn on failed attempts to add protection.

You just have to be _moderately_ real-life lucky to
turn that into five more protection improvements.

> At more realistic levels of protection, you're
> still subject to being killed by a lucky strike
> from a tough opponent, due to low HP.

Except that you aren't seeing opponents "tough in
comparison to your pets" when you keep your XL at 1,
because the game isn't generating them yet. Traps
are more of a problem than "tough opponents" for low
HP protection racketeers. This makes a =oSD the
racketeer's best friend, giving the PC the needed
time to search for traps before every step.

Being killed by "a lucky strike from a tough
opponent" is much more likely to be a problem when
you let your XL get disproportionately high in
comparison to your current AC, than when you keep
your XL at 1, because now you have tougher
opponents, ones your pet can't help fight, because
you haven't gotten down to polymorph traps yet to
upgrade your pet, and those monsters often arriving
in swarms like soldier ants, Mordor orcs, and
woodland elves, but not the AC to survive their
collective several hits per turn through your wimpy
AC.

